Lots of spoilers below.

I consider myself a medium-caliber player; I ascended my first
character last year and have 6 or 8 ascensions in total (including
valk, barb, tourist, wizard, archaeologist). Here are my thoughts.

rhapsodyindrew wrote:
> It's always been my belief that more experience levels = better chance
> of survival, mostly because of the increased HP and PW.

As others noted, higher xp level = tougher monsters generated.  For
spellcasters, higher xp level is worth it (your magic missile gets more
and more powerful).  For others, once you can do the quest (xp level 14
I think) there's no reason to advance xp levels.

> I've gotten scores above 1 million
> points, but the farthest I've ever gotten in the dungeon was the Medusa
> level - though I could probably have gone deeper if it hadn't been for
> a fatal mistake involving a damn cockatrice

Always collect lizard corpses and keep one in main inventory.  If you
can get to Medusa, you've got almost all the skills you need to ascend;
it's just a matter of learning all the YASDs and how to avoid them, and
then actually avoiding them and putting together your ascension kit.
Then only really bad luck or hurrying ahead before you're prepared are
major threats, though you can die in the Planes or via Rodney even if
you're careful.

> 1.  AC seems to be crucial, and everybody has a better AC than I do.
> So I get the idea of running straight to Minetown to buy some cheap
> protection

I hate the protection racket.  You'll get plenty of gold to buy
protection later on, and you can normally get a good enough AC to tide
you over until then via other means.  The early game is the hardest
part, and you're basically making it much harder to make the mid-game
only slightly easier if you do the protection racket.

But, make sure you buy that first level of protection as early as you
can, since you could get up to 4 AC from it (1 for later purchases).
Once you have plenty of food, clear out vaults and go get some
protection.

But some people disagree and do the racket successfully.

> but the methods for enchanting armors well eludes me.  Can
> you use a scroll of charging to increasee the enchantment on an
> armor/weapon as on a ring?  Is the general method to find a scroll of
> enchant (armor|weapon), read it, then get a magic marker and a bunch of
> blank scrolls and write away?

Early in the game, you should always be collecting things like boots,
helms, cloaks to BUCidentify and then try on.  You can often get some
decent +2/+3 stuff that way, and wasting enchant armor on items that
you're not going to keep isn't worth it.  Things that I'll enchant up
include speed boots, gauntlets of power (combat wombats) or dex
(spellcasters), helms of brilliance/telepathy, T-shirts/hawaiian
shirts, gray or silver dragon scale mail, shields of reflection (for
non-2-weaponers), and cloaks of displacement/protection/magic
resistance.

Enchant weapon I'll read early if I have even a decent weapon
(including a priest's starting mace, a longsword, silver saber, etc).
You will find tons of surplus of these by the time you finish, and they
can help you out tremendously (especially in the early game).

Always bless them before using (exception: rust/fire-proofing).

I usually wait until I have a stack of 3 EA scrolls to bless and read
them, though if my AC is really bad and holy water's not short I'll do
doubles or singles.

Magic markers I use almost exclusively for enchant armor and charging
scrolls, maybe genocide.  Bless the marker before using.

Priests can see all the blessed stuff, try it on and see if it's
enchanted (you can often get really great armor at Ludios/castle that
way.

> 2.  A big one: high XP level = bad?  So what's the preferred method -
> get a bunch of gain ability/full healing (how?) and bless with holy
> water?

Mix up potions of full healing with alchemy, and bless.  Collect big
stacks of potions to do this with.  Nurse dancing is normally not worth
it to me.  Bless it, yes.

> Bottom line: how do you approach a NetHack character, particularly in
> the early game?  I think it's time I got wise, revamped my style, and
> pushed my game to the next level.

My basic goals are (roughly in order, but not strictly)
* Find some basic armor--any helm, any shoes, any glove, any cloak that
helps AC, any shield, and mithril armor (studded leather before that,
and I keep the studded for spell casters).  I usually don't wear
anything between studded leather and mithril because of weight, but for
strong combat types I will.
* Figure out which scrolls are identify, enchant weapon, and enchant
armor (or at least which 2 are potentially EA) via price identification

The above are the "initial game" goals; probably 50% of my deaths come
before they're accomplished.  AC down to 0 is a goal, better if
possible.

* Get to Minetown and Sokoban (usually Sokoban first, since reflection
is a YAAD preventer and food may still be an issue).
* Find an altar and convert.  Sac for a decent weapon, set up a cache
of extra items.
* Really try to find magic resistance and reflection.  Sometimes not
possible, but polymorph traps can be a buzzkill.

Pause for a while here.  There's nothing wrong with spending some extra
time after clearing out Soko and Minetown, going back and forth and
making sure you have good AC and such (mithril armor is a must before
going below Minetown).

* Clear out Mine's end (usually -10 AC before doing this, hopefully
magic resistance).
* Clear out everything up to medusa, not including Ludios/Quest (though
normally I'll clear out the beginning of the Quest if possible).  Clear
out vaults and buy protection.

The above are the early mid-game goals.  Once they're accomplished, if
I don't have magic resistance/reflection then all my energy goes to
finding them.  Once that's achieved, odds are much greater than 50/50
that I'll ascend.  Medusa can be a reflection source if I don't have it
yet.  I'll do a sac-fest or similar to try to find those if I need to.

This is a good point to pause and see if you have what you need for
alchemy, enchant up armor/weapons, etc.  Basically prep-work time.

* Find levitation/water walking boots or ring of levitation
* Clear out Quest, Ludios, Medusa, and Castle in whatever order.  Pause
for prep work/take stock of surroundings after Quest/Ludios, and
Castle.  Get dragon scale mail by some means.
* Fill out the ascension kit (with the Castle wand if needed)
* Prep work again (alchemy/enchanting if needed).  Also, dig out
straight paths between up and down stairs on every level down to the
Castle.

The above are the late mid-game goals.  Once they're done, ascension is
80% or better likelihood.  Pretty much all potions of paralysis have
been stockpiled for teleporting foes in Gehennom.

* Clear out the Valley, Vlad's, and Gehennom, occasionally pausing for
more prep work (going up to your cache to do alchemy, bless up scrolls,
use magic markers, etc) as needed.  Dig out paths between all up/down
stairs.
* Find and mark the vibrating square
* Do all remaining prep work possible, take an inventory and make sure
you have everything you need to

That's the end of Gehennom.  Really make sure you're prepped and ready,
and finish up the game--things can move fast once Rodney's awoken, so
make sure you're all prepped and then take your time and don't let the
excitement rush you.

